---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import Presentation from "../../../../components/Presentation.astro";
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";

import ConceptConvergenceDiagram from "../../../../assets/courses/eng-design-fab/img/concept-convergence-diagram.svg";
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a
          href="/courses/eng-design-fab/"
          class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3"
        >
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Session 3: Design &amp; Ideation</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Launch / Concept -->
        <section id="launch">
          <Presentation>
            <section>
              <h2>Session 3</h2>
              <h3 class="text-sn-theme">Design &amp; Ideation</h3>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>You Already Have an Idea</h2>
              <p>
                Last session, we asked if you already had a solution in mind
                for your problem &mdash; before we'd even started Design.
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Jumping straight to that idea isn't Design. It's skipping
                Design and going straight to Build.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Your First Idea</h2>
              <p>Is rarely your best idea.</p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Your First Idea</h2>
              <p>It's just your <b class="text-sn-theme">fastest</b> idea.</p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Divergent Thinking</h2>
              <ul>
                <li class="fragment"><b>Quantity first</b> &mdash; aim for 3 real concepts</li>
                <li class="fragment"><b>No judging yet</b> &mdash; every idea counts while sketching</li>
                <li class="fragment">
                  <b>Genuinely different</b> &mdash; not the same idea in a
                  different color
                </li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Generate, Then Decide</h2>
              <Image
                src={ConceptConvergenceDiagram}
                alt="Diagram showing three boxes labeled Concept A, B, and C with arrows converging into a box labeled Decision Matrix, which then leads into a final box labeled Chosen Concept"
                width={720}
              />
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Sketch 3 Concepts</h2>
              <p>
                Use your Session 2 problem statement &mdash; and the sketch
                of the problem from your notebook entry &mdash; as your
                starting point.
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Three genuinely different ways you could solve it. Rough is
                fine. Fast is fine.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Now Choose: The Decision Matrix</h2>
              <p>
                Not a vote. Not a gut feeling. Score each concept against the
                same criteria, side by side.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Scoring Categories (1&ndash;3 each)</h2>
              <ul>
                <li class="fragment"><b>Cost</b></li>
                <li class="fragment"><b>Feasibility</b></li>
                <li class="fragment"><b>Time</b></li>
                <li class="fragment">
                  <b>Alignment with Success Criteria</b> &mdash; from your
                  Session 2 problem statement
                </li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Two Things to Remember</h2>
              <p><b class="text-sn-theme">Quantity isn't wasted effort.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">
                The goal of sketching three concepts is options, not a
                finished design on the first try.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Two Things to Remember</h2>
              <p><b class="text-sn-theme">The matrix doesn't decide for you.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">
                It makes your reasoning visible, so you can defend the
                concept you pick &mdash; even if it isn't the flashiest one.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Next Time</h2>
              <p>Session 4: Makerspace Safety I</p>
              <p class="fragment">
                You've picked a direction. Before anyone touches a tool, we
                cover how to stay safe doing it.
              </p>
            </section>
          </Presentation>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Build</h2>

          <h3>Step 1: Sketch 3 Concepts</h3>
          <p>
            Starting from your Session 2 problem statement, sketch <b
              >three genuinely different</b
            > ways you could solve it. Follow these ground rules:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><b class="text-sn-theme">Quantity first</b> &mdash; get to 3 real concepts</li>
            <li><b class="text-sn-theme">No judging yet</b> &mdash; don't rule anything out while sketching</li>
            <li>
              <b class="text-sn-theme">Genuinely different</b> &mdash; different
              mechanisms or approaches, not the same idea with a color swap
            </li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Step 2: Build Your Decision Matrix</h3>
          <p>
            Score each of your 3 concepts, 1 (weakest) to 3 (strongest), on
            each category. Here's a worked example for a different problem
            &mdash; a backpack strap that digs into your shoulder:
          </p>

          <div class="text-start">
            <table class="table table-dark table-sm">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Concept</th>
                  <th>Cost</th>
                  <th>Feasibility</th>
                  <th>Time</th>
                  <th>Alignment</th>
                  <th>Total</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td>A: Add extra padding</td>
                  <td>3</td>
                  <td>3</td>
                  <td>3</td>
                  <td>2</td>
                  <td><b class="text-sn-theme">11</b></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>B: Widen the strap</td>
                  <td>2</td>
                  <td>2</td>
                  <td>2</td>
                  <td>3</td>
                  <td>9</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>C: Swiveling attachment point</td>
                  <td>1</td>
                  <td>1</td>
                  <td>1</td>
                  <td>3</td>
                  <td>6</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p>
            Notice: Concept C tied for the best <i>alignment</i> score, but
            came in with the lowest total &mdash; it's one of the most direct
            fixes, but also the most expensive and hardest to build in the
            time you have. The matrix surfaces that trade-off instead of
            hiding it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Now build your own matrix for your own 3 concepts, scored against
            your own Session 2 success criteria.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Checkpoint</h2>

          <p>Before you move on, check your work against these two questions.</p>

          <div class="text-start">
            <dl>
              <dt>Are your 3 concepts genuinely different?</dt>
              <dd>
                If you covered up the labels, could someone still tell them
                apart as different approaches &mdash; not just different
                colors or sizes of the same idea?
              </dd>
              <dt>Did you score honestly?</dt>
              <dd>
                Scoring your favorite concept a 3 across every category,
                every time, is a sign you picked first and scored second
                &mdash; not the other way around.
              </dd>
            </dl>
          </div>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Reflection -->
        <section id="reflection">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>

          <p>Answer these on your own. Reflections are individual, even when the rest of the session was team work.</p>

          <ol>
            <li>Which concept scored highest? Does that match which one you'd have picked by gut feeling alone? If not, what's the difference?</li>
            <li>Does your chosen concept require any tool or material you haven't used before? Session 4 starts shop safety — it's worth knowing now what you'll need to learn.</li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>Submit your 3 concept sketches, your decision matrix, and your reflection.</p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Activity Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
